# Line endings are a repository property, not a per-developer one. Without
# this file the working tree depends on each machine's core.autocrlf setting,
# which is how reports/*/data/*.csv came to be CRLF on Windows and broke
# `npm run report:charts`: the CSV reader split on \n, every row kept a
# trailing \r, and it landed on the last column name -- silently renaming
# `finger_number` to `finger_number\r` so every lookup returned undefined.
#
# `eol=lf` is the load-bearing half, not the bare `text=auto`: `text=auto`
# only normalizes to LF in the object database, and still checks CRLF back
# out wherever core.autocrlf says to. Pinning the working tree to LF on
# every platform keeps two classes of bug from returning:
#   - parsers that split on \n and keep a trailing \r (the CSV bug above)
#   - `npm run format:check`, which compares line endings as part of
#     formatting, so a CRLF worktree fails locally while CI passes on LF
* text=auto eol=lf

# Read by scripts and by SQLite. These are forced `text` rather than left to
# `text=auto`'s binary sniffing, so a parser never has to defend against a
# stray \r even if the file picks up an odd byte.
*.csv text eol=lf
*.tsv text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf

# Never touch: the count-in samples and the fingering reference scan. These
# come after the catch-all so `binary` (-text -diff) wins.
*.mp3 binary
*.png binary
*.db binary
